    "body": "I don't know if the issue is the same for you or not, but I've also been having a hard time getting a command to execute using powershell. After much digging and using the \"trace\" logging suggested above (very helpful!), I discovered that the reason mine isn't working is that the working directory when the command is being executed is _not_ my current directory! Look:\r\n```toml\r\n[custom.test]\r\ncommand = \"pwd\"\r\nfiles = [\".gitignore\"]\r\n```\r\n```\r\nDevelopment\\MyApp Path\r\n----\r\nC:\\Users\\me\r\n❯\r\n```\r\nFor completeness: when I opened a powershell terminal, I started in my home directory, and that's where the command thinks I am when it executes. However, if I run `powershell` in the powershell terminal, then everything seems okay:\r\n```\r\nDevelopment\\MyApp Path\r\n----\r\nC:\\Users\\me\r\n❯ powershell\r\nWindows PowerShell\r\nCopyright (C) Microsoft Corporation. All rights reserved.\r\n\r\nTry the new cross-platform PowerShell https://aka.ms/pscore6\r\n\r\nLoading personal and system profiles took 888ms.\r\n\r\nDevelopment\\MyApp Path\r\n----\r\nC:\\Development\\MyApp\r\n❯\r\n```\r\nMaybe you can try and see if your path is wrong like mine? It would explain your finding that it seems the current directory isn't being treated as a git repo.",
